//? Title: Rearranging calls through two middle switches
//? Subtitle: Actual bipartite edge-color assignments and conflict-free link certificates
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Route a restricted call set through two middle switches without reusing an
input-to-middle or middle-to-output link. Calls form a bipartite multigraph
whose endpoint degrees are at most two. Actual native BFS assignments explain a
small path and retain the original four-cycle, while an independent feasibility
check certifies the resulting link choices.
)

\toc

> The restricted routing model

An input group and an output group identify each call. Its middle-switch choice
is0 or1. Calls sharing an input must choose different middles, and calls sharing
an output must choose different middles. Thus the choices are a
two-edge-coloring of the bipartite call graph. Parallel calls between the same
groups are allowed and require opposite colors.

A finite graph of maximum degree two has components that are isolated vertices,
paths or cycles. Only vertices incident to calls matter here. Bipartite cycles
have even length, so alternating two colors works on each path and cycle.
Starting a new component at color zero and propagating opposite colors through
shared endpoints therefore produces a feasible assignment. Component-wise color
flips are equally valid; the particular numeric choices are implementation
identities.

This argument concerns the supplied degree-at-most-two model. It is not a
theorem that an arbitrary Clos network with two middle switches is strictly
nonblocking. Rearrangement may change existing calls’ middle choices; admitting
new calls online without moving existing paths is a different requirement.

> A three-call path, one actual assignment at a time

The small path calls(0,0),(1,0),(1,1) must alternate0,1,0. Native records
identify the actual discovering call and whether it shared an input or output.
The next diagram follows the actual first-assignment order, and the complete
route vector retains original call order. The feasibility checker verifies link
constraints without insisting on the producer’s specific color orientation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/switch_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/nonblocking_switch.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, std::size_t>> calls{{0, 0}, {1, 0},
                                                           {1, 1}};
    auto t = dm::algorithms::nonblocking_switch_trace(2, 2, calls);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    std::print("| actual assigned call | actual middle | actual component | discovery |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.assignments.size(); ++i) {
        auto &a = t.assignments[i];
        auto route =
            a.component_start ? std::string("component start")
            : std::format("call {} via {}", a.parent,
                          a.via_input ? "input" : "output");
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", a.call, a.middle, a.component,
                   route);
        d.node(std::to_string(i), {7. * i, 0},
               std::format("Actual call {}\nmiddle {}", a.call, a.middle));
        if (i)
            d.edge(std::to_string(i - 1), std::to_string(i));
    }
    std::print("\nActual original-order routes {}; feasible certificate {}.\n",
               t.analysis.routes,
               dm::algorithms::nonblocking_switch_feasible(2, 2, calls,
                                                           t.analysis.routes));
    d.save_svg("build/switch_tiny.svg");
}
```
